Z||000000||shiloh187||04-08-2001||07:41 PM||||Okay guys here is another one for you...<P>i am redoing my interior and notice that everything is held on by different screws in my car..  some are star screws, others regular screws, others long screws, and the seatblet is a huge stra screw.  Also others need rathchets.  Is there a way to get uniform bolts on the interior?<P>second thing is the timing chain.  I am reading my haynes and it says 25k miles... every 25k miles i need to spend a few hundred to have this replaced?<P>thanks<BR>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroLisa||04-08-2001||08:09 PM||||The interior is done that way so you loose all your tools while trying to figure out which one works where  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I went through the same annoyance not too long ago.||||reg||1
Z||000002||shiloh187||04-08-2001||10:26 PM||||is this correct about the timing belt?<P>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||04-08-2001||10:29 PM||||Jeez I hope not about the timing chain!  Mine has probably 140,000 miles on it for what I know!<P>This a V6 or L4?<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bob Englert||04-08-2001||10:35 PM||||FieroJoe:<P>If you have 140K on a 4 cyl, I would strongly suggest that you consider replacing the timing gear.  They are phelonic (plastic) and have a nasty habit of breaking without any warning.  The metal timing gears are only listed for the 84-86 cars but you might be able to use one if you also change the crank gear.  If I was doing an engine rebuild this would be a high priority.<P>Just my opinion....<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000005||shiloh187||04-09-2001||12:04 AM||||mine is an 87 coupe 4 cylinder... with 70k miles on it..  <P>shiloh||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||04-09-2001||01:54 AM||||After more than 100,000 miles the chain does get pretty badly stretched out. Ive not heard of timing chain that has broken without some pretty serious hard launching. <P>25 k seems to be a bit soon. I would say it was optimal but by no means essential!<P>As for the timing gear on the 4 cyl, when the 87 coupe came up for a rebuild my engine shop had just the steel gear for it. I don't have the part number but it didn't seem to be any big deal when I asked for it. And it sure doesn't sound any louder than a 4 cylinder gets!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||04-09-2001||02:59 AM||||So many times haynes and chiltons books are generic.<BR>When the talk about changing timing componets<BR>every 25K miles, they refer to BELTS, not chains or gears. Ingnore that statement, good idea to replace geared componets (meshed or chained) every 100-125K.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrgone||04-09-2001||05:23 AM||||how about my 86 2m4 with 240,000k's ?||||reg||4
Z||000009||Cozmo||04-09-2001||07:41 AM||||My Duke just ate up it's timing gear, just rolled over 100K miles... No symptoms or warnings it just died.  It is a real bear to change, wait till it tears up (if ever) then change them. BTW, the metal gears work fine on an '87 even if Melling says they may not work.<P>------------------<BR><B>C02M0</B><BR><I>Suncoast Fieros</I><BR>'85 2M6 Targa<BR>'86 GT<BR>'87 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000010||My7Fieros||04-09-2001||04:36 PM||||As far as the interior goes, most everything is held on by either a T-15 torx or a 7 mm socket....the seatbelt bolt up top is a T-47 I believe. Torx head drivers or bits are available at any hardware store.<BR>The part about changing your timing chain at 25k miles is bs...disregard. M7F ||||reg||1
